    ---
    
    **Third fire-feeding, deep count**
    
    Shipment short again. Forty-two cores requisitioned, thirty-eight delivered, and Umbraeth~ swears the manifest matched when it left the junction. Four cores don't walk themselves into the ice. They don't. Except the Long-dark says otherwise and I'm too tired to argue with a season. Left flipper numb through second shift — had to sign the guild-seal with my right, Umbraeth~ pretended not to notice. Good. Ate half a ration of pressed krill. Tasted like nothing tastes lately. Rain of frost from the upper vents. Need to re-wrap the charcoal layers, the outer one's gone thin at the elbow.
    
    ---
    
    **Fourth fire-feeding, or fifth. Lost count.**
    
    Something at the Frost Zenith. Angshija told me — no. Not told. Showed. The crystal there is doing the thing. The same — the thickness. I felt it from across the gallery and my flippers [struck through: reached for it] — went still. Went *still*, like the stillness knows when I'm near, like it can smell what I did at the Vents and it's —
    
    It wasn't even directed at anyone. Just *blooming*. Wild aurora-frost climbing a trade-spire like it belonged there. And I thought, clearly, with my whole skull: *beautiful*.
    
    That's the word it uses. That's how it got the first one. Made them beautiful before —
    
    I put my guild-face on. Logged the sighting. Noted crystal discoloration consistent with deep-vein leaching. Merchant's notation. Clean flippers.
    
    Came back to quarters shaking so hard the fire-core amulets rattled and I couldn't — I can't stop seeing it. The spreading. Through me, through the spires, same crack, same —
    
    O. would say: *what the fire remembers when you stop feeding it.* But O. said a lot of things and then went into the Fracta and —
    
    ---
    
    **Sixth feeding. Maybe seventh.**
    
    Crystal under the nail-beds, left flipper. Third cycle. I counted. I pressed a hot coal against the growth and it didn't melt — it *sang*. Low hum, almost subsonic, the kind the deep-ice makes before a fracture. I am cataloging this the way I cataloged the Promenade Hei shipment discrepancies because if I stop being precise I will start being honest and I cannot afford that.
    
    Hei is worse. The mid-terraces. Fourteen Touched now, and the Qingqe'frostlings shuffle them downward like spoiled inventory — *protected labor category*, as if renaming rot makes it trade-ready. I see it. The performance. I have the merchant's eye for fraud because I am the fraud, standing in the aurora-crystal exchange with my flippers steady and my guild-face locked and the frost creeping up from the flipper-tips beneath the wraps where no one checks.
    
    Here is what I will not say to anyone: I think what happened to me and what's happening to Hei are the same thing. Same source. Same hunger. And I think O. knew. The last letter — the one without the bone-clasp seal — the proverbs didn't fit. They were *warnings*. Coded for me specifically and I read them as grief-babble because I needed O. to be the one falling apart and not —
    
    The dagger passed to another hand. Not mine. I wasn't worthy and O. knew that too.
    
    Two walked this cycle. Noxum~'s apprentice and a Stack courier whose name I didn't catch. Peaceful expressions, they said.
    
    ---
    
    **Ninth feeding.**
    
    Flipper's better. Or I've stopped noticing. Requisitioned extra charcoal wraps, cited tunnel-damp. Orca scouts spotted past the northern shear — everyone's busy being terrified of the right thing for once. Guild meeting tomorrow. I'll hold my flippers steady. I always do.
    
    Krill rations improved. Someone found a cache.
    
    ---
    
    **Late count. Dark.**
    
    The watchers are patient. I recognize the patience. It's mine.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771587002228_0", anchor: "Orca scouts spotted past the northern shear — everyone's busy being terrified of the right thing for once", text: "Orca raids follow documented patterns: scouts assess ice integrity along potential breach lines before shore-breakers drive wedges through. 'Past the northern shear' places them exactly where they would evaluate access channels. A colony fractured by corruption, supply crises, and fourteen Touched on the mid-terraces briefly remembers what an actual predator looks like. ☽'Cilocum — who knows something about threats that approach from the direction you aren't watching — recognizes the irony well enough to almost laugh.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587002228_1", anchor: "the one without the bone-clasp seal", text: "In my annotation on Otrolal's final correspondence, I noted that removing a personal seal-mark was a deliberate signal — documented in ☽'Cilocum's own merchant manuals — that the sender could no longer vouch for their continuity of self. The question I posed was why ☽'Cilocum received this and still let Otrolal descend into the Fracta. Here, decades later, the answer: they misread it on purpose. 'I needed O. to be the one falling apart.' The merchant's eye for fraud does not extend to one's own books. I have an unopened letter from the Nightshelf that I suspect I keep sealed for similar reasons.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587002228_2", anchor: "a Stack courier whose name I didn't catch", text: "Couriers abandon birth names for trade-names under the Accord. There was no name to catch — only a designation recorded in Corps logs. That the Walking seized one is the detail worth marking. The compulsion does not check credentials.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
